=== layout: one-panel

# Nearer or Farther?

@body

Band numbers increase as we move *away from the centromere*.

{table-style=comparison}
| Same Arm | Nearer the Centromere | Farther from the Centromere |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Chromosome 14, q arm | 14q21 | 14q22 |

- Compare chromosome and arm first
- Then compare region, band, and subband
- A band label is an address, not a distance in cM

@citation

https://medlineplus.gov/genetics/understanding/howgeneswork/genelocation/

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

For chromosome *17q*, which band lies most distal (farthest from the centromere)?

- A. 17q36
- B. 17q36.3
- C. 17q11
- D. 17q34.1
- E. 17q41.3

*Use these schematic labels to practice the numbering rule.*

@answer

E. 17q41.3

=== layout: one-panel

# Band Order: Answer

@body

- Same chromosome and arm in every choice: *17q*
- Compare the region first: *4* is farther out than *3* or *1*
- Only *17q41.3* is in region 4
- [*Answer: E. 17q41.3*]{color=#007A00}

These are schematic labels, not a map of verified human chromosome 17 bands.

=== layout: section

# Reading a Karyotype

=== layout: one-panel

# Three Parts of a Simple Karyotype

@body

{table-style=comparison text-align=left}
| Part | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| 47 | Number of chromosomes |
| XY | Sex-chromosome complement |
| +21 | Extra copy of chromosome 21 |

- +21 means an extra *whole chromosome 21*
- This is different from an address such as 7q31.2

@citation

https://medlineplus.gov/genetics/condition/down-syndrome/

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

What does *47,XY,+21* describe?

- A. XX with two extra copies of chromosome 21
- B. XY with a partial deletion of chromosome 21
- C. XY with an extra pair of chromosome 21
- D. XX with one extra copy of chromosome 21
- E. XY with one extra copy of chromosome 21

@answer

E. XY with one extra copy of chromosome 21

=== layout: one-panel

# Karyotype: Answer

@body

- *47*: one more than the usual 46 chromosomes
- *XY*: the sex-chromosome complement
- *+21*: one extra copy of chromosome 21
- [*Answer: E*]{color=#007A00}
- Three copies of chromosome 21: *trisomy 21*

The homework may put the change before the sex chromosomes: *47,+21,XY*. It describes the same example.

=== layout: reference

# For Reference: A-G Groups

@body

- Reference: not covered in lecture
- Seven older chromosome groups, A-G, based on size and shape

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10c-chromo_nomenclature/image_008.png)

=== layout: reference

# For Reference: X and Y

@body

- Reference: not covered in lecture
- X is in group C; Y is in group G
- The SRY gene is on the short arm of Y

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10c-chromo_nomenclature/image_009.png)

=== layout: reference

# For Reference: Address Ranges

@body

- Reference: not covered in lecture
- A single band: *17q12*
- A range of bands: *17q12-q21*
- A range gives a broader location when the position is less precisely known
- Decimal digits name finer subbands; they are not decimal distances

=== layout: bookend

# Key Takeaways

@body

- p is the short arm; q is the long arm
- Read an address: chromosome, arm, region, band, subband
- On the same arm, band numbers increase away from the centromere
- Read a simple karyotype: total, sex chromosomes, chromosome change

